---
/**
 * LoanAndPlacement — the two amounts that sit outside the package totals,
 * /pricing only.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SECTION EXISTS TO CORRECT. The site used to say that further
 * placement-related fees "may apply", which a reader takes to mean a cost
 * the employer bears, possibly, on top of the package. Both halves of that
 * are wrong. Core-pages design spec §2.4, binding on all copy: the
 * employer ADVANCES the loan and the placement fee and RECOVERS them
 * through the helper's repayment. It is ultimately the HELPER'S cost. Copy
 * must state both halves — the money leaves the employer's account first,
 * and it comes back — because either half alone is a different and untrue
 * claim.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND MUST STAY ABSENT.
 *
 * DirectHired supplied the repayment mechanics on 2026-08-16: the range it
 * runs over, where in the helper's pay it comes from, and what she
 * receives alongside it. None of that is published here, on any other
 * page, or in any FAQ answer. It describes a salary-deduction arrangement
 * on a licensed employment agency's public website, in an area MOM
 * regulates closely, and source brief §19 requires the final wording to be
 * reviewed against DirectHired's actual commercial and regulatory terms
 * before it goes out. It is the highest-liability sentence on the site.
 *
 * The gap is recorded as a declared input in
 * scripts/generate-info-required.mjs (Category C of
 * docs/INFORMATION-REQUIRED-BEFORE-PRODUCTION.md) and enforced by
 * tests/compliance-gate.test.ts, which scans the built HTML. What is
 * missing is sign-off, not information.
 *
 * A PARAPHRASE IS A BREACH. Writing "over several months", "from what she
 * earns", or "she keeps her rest-day pay" would publish the same
 * arrangement in different words and would pass a literal search for the
 * supplied phrases. Do not gesture at the mechanism. The page is complete
 * and honest without it: a family needs to know who ultimately bears the
 * cost, which is stated here in full.
 *
 * The loan carries no figure and no range — spec §2.3 says it is genuinely
 * case-by-case and §7 forbids publishing a range. "It depends" is the
 * true answer, so it is given as the answer rather than softened into an
 * approximate one.
 */
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro'
import Card from '../components/Card.astro'
---

<section class="loan-placement">
  <Container>
    <SectionHeader
      title="The loan and the placement fee"
      lede="Two amounts sit outside the package totals above. They are the ones most often misunderstood, so here is what they are and who ends up paying them."
    />

    <div class="loan-grid">
      <Card>
        <div class="amount-card">
          <h3>Placement fee</h3>
          <p class="amount-figure">One month’s salary</p>
          <p>
            Fixed at one month’s salary, and the same amount whether you take on a new helper or a
            transfer helper. There is no separate scale to work through.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Card>

      <Card>
        <div class="amount-card">
          <h3>Helper loan</h3>
          <p class="amount-figure">Assessed case by case</p>
          <p>
            The loan depends on the individual helper, so there is no standard figure to publish —
            and we would rather publish none than one that turns out not to be yours. Our team goes
            through the exact amount for the helper you choose before you commit to anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <div class="who-pays">
      <h3 class="who-pays-heading">Who actually bears these</h3>
      <p>
        You advance both amounts at the start, and you recover them through the helper’s repayment.
        Both halves matter: the money does leave your account first, and it does come back. They
        are ultimately the <strong>helper’s</strong> cost, not an additional charge you carry on
        top of the package.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .loan-placement {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* Cream, so the two Cards below read as raised against it — the same
       reasoning PricingSection.astro records for block 06. Also puts this
       section between the white replacement block above and the white FAQ
       below, keeping the page's grounds alternating. */
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .loan-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .amount-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .amount-card h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .amount-figure {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    /* Deliberately the same display treatment PricingCard gives a package
       total. "One month's salary" and "assessed case by case" ARE the
       answers to "how much?" — setting them as prose while every real
       number on the page is set as a figure would read as evasion. */
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .amount-card p:not(.amount-figure) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .who-pays {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .who-pays-heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .who-pays p {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    .loan-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
  }
</style>
